& (972 - 268)(973 - 267)(974 - 266) \\
&\qquad+ (968 - 272)(967 - 273)(966 - 274) - (999 - 222)^3
\end{align*}

ref_completion:
We want to find an integer $n$ such that $160n \equiv 1 \pmod{1399}$. This is equivalent to finding integers $n$ and $k$ such that $160n + 1399k = 1$. We can use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor of 160 and 1399 and express it as a linear combination of 160 and 1399.

\begin{align*} 1399 &= 8 \cdot 160 + 119 \\ 160 &= 1 \cdot 119 + 41 \\ 119 &= 2 \cdot 41 + 37 \\ 41 &= 1 \cdot 37 + 4 \\ 37 &= 9 \cdot 4 + 1 \\ 4 &= 4 \cdot 1 + 0 \end{align*}
The greatest common divisor of 160 and 1399 is 1. Now we work backwards to express 1 as a linear combination of 160 and 1399.
\begin{align*} 1 &= 37 - 9 \cdot 4 \\ 1 &= 37 - 9 (41 - 1 \cdot 37) \\ 1 &= 37 - 9 \cdot 41 + 9 \cdot 37 \\ 1 &= 10 \cdot 37 - 9 \cdot 41 \\ 1 &= 10 (119 - 2 \cdot 41) - 9 \cdot 41 \\ 1 &= 10 \cdot 119 - 20 \cdot 41 - 9 \cdot 41 \\ 1 &= 10 \cdot 119 - 29 \cdot 41 \\ 1 &= 10 \cdot 119 - 29 (160 - 1 \cdot 119) \\ 1 &= 10 \cdot 119 - 29 \cdot 160 + 29 \cdot 119 \\ 1 &= 39 \cdot 119 - 29 \cdot 160 \\ 1 &= 39 (1399 - 8 \cdot 160) - 29 \cdot 160 \\ 1 &= 39 \cdot 1399 - 312 \cdot 160 - 29 \cdot 160 \\ 1 &= 39 \cdot 1399 - 341 \cdot 160 \end{align*}
Thus, we have $1 = 39 \cdot 1399 - 341 \cdot 160$. This means that $-341 \cdot 160 \equiv 1 \pmod{1399}$. We want to find an integer $n$ such that $0 \leq n < 1399$. We have $n \equiv -341 \pmod{1399}$.
So $n = -341 + 1399 = 1058$.
Therefore, $160 \cdot 1058 \equiv 1 \pmod{1399}$.

Final Answer: \boxed{1058}

